{
  "raw": {
    "https://employment.plu.edu/postings/8903": {
      "title": "Visiting Assistant Professor/Instructor of Biology",
      "description": "PLU - Applicant Portal | Visiting Assistant Professor/Instructor of Biology Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help PLU Home Visiting Assistant Professor/Instructor of Biology Below you will find the details for the position including any supplementary documentation and questions you should review before applying for the opening. Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Applicants should submit the following: Cover letter; Curriculum vitae; List of three references; Statement of teaching philosophy in which you describe your goals for student learning, the methods you use to help students achieve them, and how you assess student learning. This statement should explicitly address how you will create an inclusive learning environment that is attuned to diversity and equity. For questions or more information, please contact Amy Siegesmund at siegesam@plu.edu Posting Details Posting Details Posting Number: 0603127 Recruitment Type: Open to All Applicants Position Title: Visiting Assistant Professor/Instructor of Biology Position Type: Faculty Benefits Status: Benefits Eligible FLSA: Exempt Hiring Range: $29,000 - $33,640 per year + benefits package Location Tacoma, WA 98447 Department: Biology Department Posting Date: 09/03/2026 Closing Date Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. Work Schedule: Full-time position available for Spring semester only beginning February 1, 2027. General Description: The Biology Department at Pacific Lutheran University is seeking applicants who are committed to excellence in teaching for a full-time position for the Spring 2027 semester only. The successful applicant will teach introductory microbiology (lecture + 3 labs) for pre-nursing students. The biology department is a vibrant, student-centered academic community committed to helping students understand the complexity and diversity of life while developing the skills to think critically, ask meaningful questions, and engage in scientific discovery. Our faculty represent a broad range of biological disciplines, including molecular and cellular biology, microbiology, anatomy and physiology, developmental biology, ecology, and evolution. We emphasize learning biology by doing biology, with hands-on laboratory and field experiences, undergraduate research, and close faculty-student mentoring woven throughout the curriculum. Our students pursue diverse pathways into health professions, graduate and professional programs, research, environmental science, education, and other careers in the life sciences. We are looking for an individual who identifies first and foremost as an educator and is dedicated to using effective and inclusive teaching methods and practices. Our students come from various backgrounds with different values and beliefs. Many of them are first-generation college students, non-traditional students, students of color, and LGBTQ students. We particularly welcome applications from individuals who can facilitate discussions across diverse perspectives and who share our commitment to promoting diversity, inclusion, and equity in their teaching approaches. Essential Functions and Responsibilities: Teach assigned courses at the undergraduate level. Hold regular student hours. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: Solid background in microbiology. Knowledge of effective and inclusive teaching and learning strategies. Commitment to working effectively with a diverse faculty and student body. Demonstrated enthusiasm for teaching at the undergraduate level within a liberal arts setting. Ability to communicate effectively. Required Qualifications: Ph.D. in biology or related discipline must be in hand by February 1, 2027 for appointment at the Visiting Assistant Professor level. ABD or MS in biology or related discipline required by February 1, 2027 for appointment at the Instructor level. Applicant must be legally eligible to work in the United States on hire date AND legally authorized to work in the United States for Pacific Lutheran University without sponsorship. Preferred Qualifications: Previous teaching experience at the undergraduate level. Work Conditions: Physical Requirements: Special Instructions to Applicants: Applicants should submit the following: Cover letter; Curriculum vitae; List of three references; Statement of teaching philosophy in which you describe your goals for student learning, the methods you use to help students achieve them, and how you assess student learning. This statement should explicitly address how you will create an inclusive learning environment that is attuned to diversity and equity. For questions or more information, please contact Amy Siegesmund at siegesam@plu.edu Other Information: Pacific Lutheran University purposefully integrates the liberal arts, professional studies and civic engagement in the beautiful Pacific Northwest. Our faculty lead with a bold commitment to expanding well-being, opportunity, and justice and instill this commitment in their students. PLU educators work to create an environment where students feel heard and valued, nurturing lifelong learners who aren’t afraid to do things differently to make the world better for everyone. PLU supports faculty as they help students turn ambition into purpose, skills into tools, and their caring into a transformative force. Because PLU is a community committed to the creation and advancement of knowledge through diversity and inclusion, the university actively recruits a faculty, staff, and student body from all religions, races, socioeconomic groups, sexual orientations, and gender expressions and from all over the world. For the 2025-26 academic year, 45 percent of PLU undergraduate students are from the first generation in their families to attend college; 51.4 percent self-identify as students of color; and 34.2 percent are Pell Grant–eligible. Offering distinctive opportunities for global engagement, collaborative student-faculty research, and purposeful learning, our faculty guide 2,800 students from all faiths and backgrounds to discern their purpose through relevant coursework, inclusive mentoring, and internships at world-class Puget Sound-area businesses and institutions. PLU is located on a 156-acre woodland campus in the Parkland neighborhood of Tacoma, Washington and about 40 minutes south of downtown Seattle. The campus reflects consciously green practices and is in proximity to the natural beauty of Mount Rainier, the Cascade and Olympic mountain ranges, and the Puget Sound. PLU administration supports and respects the strong culture of faculty-shared governance by working closely and collaboratively with faculty leadership to sustain and strengthen existing structures and procedures while working together on key institutional, academic and curricular decisions. PLU is an equal opportunity employer and welcomes applications from members of historically underrepresented and marginalized groups, women, veterans, persons with disabilities, and others who would bring broadly diverse perspectives, experiences, and backgrounds in educational, research, or other work activities. For general application questions or if you need accommodations at any point in the application and/or interview process, please contact the Human Resources Office at 253-535-7185 or humr@plu.edu . Click here for detailed benefits information. Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Will you have a Master's degree or higher in hand by the time of appointment? Yes No * Are you legally eligible to work in the United States on your hire date AND legally authorized to work in the United States for Pacific Lutheran University without sponsorship? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Teaching Philosophy List of Three References Optional Documents Copyright © 2019 Pacific Lutheran University. All rights reserved. 12180 Park Ave. S Tacoma, WA 98447 (253) 535-7185 www.plu.edu To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://employment.plu.edu/postings/8884": {
      "title": "Credential Specialist (2 positions)",
      "description": "PLU - Applicant Portal | Credential Specialist (2 positions) Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help PLU Home Credential Specialist (2 positions) Below you will find the details for the position including any supplementary documentation and questions you should review before applying for the opening. Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Posting Details Posting Number: 0603113 Recruitment Type: Open to All Applicants Position Title: Credential Specialist (2 positions) Position Type: Staff Benefits Status: Benefits Eligible FLSA: Non-Exempt Hiring Range: $21.50-$24.25 per hour, plus excellent benefits. Location Tacoma, WA 98447. Department: Office of Academic Records Posting Date: 08/12/2026 Closing Date When a sufficient number of qualified applicants have been identified. Work Schedule: Monday – Friday, 8am – 5pm. Possibility of limited remote work. General Description: The Credential Specialist provides high quality service as administrative support for the Office of Academic Records with an emphasis on credit evaluations of prospective and current students, development of course equivalency guide, and student record maintenance. This position also applies transfer credit updates for current students, processes course equivalencies, and supports other key unit functions. Reports to the Assistant Registrar for Transfer and Articulation. Essential Functions and Responsibilities: Support student credit evaluations by reviewing transcripts through researching course work, accreditation, degree programs, grade conversions and credit translations. Accurately apply verified transfer credit to student records and degree audits ( CAPP ). Assist in developing and maintaining course equivalency data in Banner, TES , and ProcessMaker. Act as a resource for students, faculty, and staff on policies and procedures relating to transfer. Proactively grow transfer equivalency data for common transfer partner institutions and BSN prerequisites. Partner with the Center for Student Success and Admission, traditional and nursing, to support new and current student registration with timely credit evaluation. Work in coordination with the Academic Records Evaluation Team to ensure all transcript evaluation is completed. Assist in the review and awarding of accelerated BSN and graduate degrees. Serve as backup for other records services functions, as well as responding to general inquiries as needed. Perform other duties as assigned. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: Ability to be thorough, detail oriented, and professional. Excellent customer service, communication, and interpersonal skills. Strong organizational, administrative, and problem-solving skills. Ability to work effectively both independently and as a team member. Ability to serve a diverse array of students and other PLU constituents. Skills in Microsoft Office, Google Apps for Education, and a willingness to learn new technologies and processes. Required Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree OR 2 years of higher education/academic administration experience. Education may substitute for some experience. High school diploma or GED . Finalist applicants must satisfactorily complete pre-employment background checks. Preferred Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree. Experience with higher education systems (e.g., Banner, TES ). Familiarity with academic transfer policy and processes. Work Conditions: Physical Requirements: Special Instructions to Applicants: Other Information: PLU is a small, private university where caring means more than kindness and consideration — it means a bold commitment to expanding well-being, opportunity, and justice. Because PLU is a community committed to the creation and advancement of knowledge through diversity and inclusion, the university actively recruits a diverse faculty, staff, and student body. PLU is an equal opportunity employer and welcomes applications from members of historically underrepresented and minoritized groups, women, veterans, persons with disabilities, and others who would bring broadly diverse perspectives, experiences, and backgrounds in educational, research or other work activities. PLU offers an excellent benefits package including tuition remission for employees and their dependents, retirement plan, medical, dental, paid vacation and sick leave, major holidays off (including Christmas/New Year’s break), additional flexible time off, and many other great university benefits. PLU is a qualifying employer under the Public Service Loan Forgiveness Program as a 501©(3) tax-exempt organization. Click here for detailed benefits information. For general application questions or if you need accommodations at any point in the application and/or interview process, please contact the Human Resources Office at 253-535-7185 or humr@plu.edu . Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Do you have a High School Diploma or GED? Yes No * Are you 18 years of age or older? Yes No * Are you legally eligible to work in the United States on your hire date AND legally authorized to work in the United States for Pacific Lutheran University without sponsorship now and indefinitely into the future? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter Resume Optional Documents List of Three References Copyright © 2019 Pacific Lutheran University. All rights reserved. 12180 Park Ave. S Tacoma, WA 98447 (253) 535-7185 www.plu.edu To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://employment.plu.edu/postings/8738": {
      "title": "PLU",
      "description": "PLU - Applicant Portal | Nursing Faculty (Part-time) - Tacoma Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help PLU Home Nursing Faculty (Part-time) - Tacoma Below you will find the details for the position including any supplementary documentation and questions you should review before applying for the opening. Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Applications are considered throughout the academic year and reviewed based on programmatic needs. If a review of your application indicates a match with our current needs, you will be contacted by phone or email for an interview. Applicants should submit a resume/cv by clicking on the “Apply” link above. Clearly indicate formal education and areas of practice expertise, as well as any specialized training and/or relevant certifications. For more information, please contact SarahAnn McFadden at mcfaddsm@plu.edu Posting Details Posting Details Posting Number: 0603036 Recruitment Type: Open to All Applicants Position Title: Nursing Faculty (Part-time) - Tacoma Position Type: Faculty Benefits Status: Not Benefits Eligible FLSA: Exempt Hiring Range: $3,400 per WTU for MSN; $4,000 per WTU for DNP. Location Tacoma, WA 98447 Department: Nursing, School of Posting Date: 04/29/2026 Closing Date Review of application materials will begin immediately and will remain open until filled. Work Schedule: Part-time; schedule is determined by the clinical placement need. Clinicals can be Monday-Tuesday-Wednesday-Thursday-Friday-Saturday or Sunday, 8, 10, or 12 hours shifts, and day, evening, or night shift depending on the clinical placement. General Description: PLU maintains a part-time nursing faculty pool in all areas of nursing specialties (Geriatrics, Medical/Surgical, Maternity, Mental Health, Pediatrics, and Simulation) for available assignments during the academic year. The PLU School of Nursing is dedicated to fostering a diverse and inclusive academic environment. We actively encourage applications from individuals whose backgrounds or interests align with this commitment. For over 70 years, the PLU School of Nursing has been preparing BSN graduates, and for more than two decades, it has educated graduate nurses. Our graduates are highly regarded throughout the Puget Sound region, demonstrated by a 100% employment rate within six months of graduation. Essential Functions and Responsibilities: Clinical Instruction and Supervision Provide hands-on instruction and supervision to nursing students in clinical settings, ensuring the safe delivery of patient care and application of nursing theory to practice. Student Performance Evaluation Observe, assess, and provide timely, constructive feedback on students’ clinical skills, critical thinking, professionalism, assignments, and adherence to ethical and safety standards. Role Modeling and Mentorship Serve as a professional role model by demonstrating best practices in nursing care, communication, and teamwork; support students’ growth in confidence and competence. Provide coaching and mentoring for students outside of class time if they need additional support or instruction to be successful. Collaboration with Faculty and Clinical Staff Maintain open communication with ABSN program coordinator, course coordinators, full-time faculty, and healthcare facility staff to align clinical experiences with course outcomes. Documentation and Compliance Accurately document student attendance, clinical hours, and performance evaluations in accordance with program policies and regulatory requirements (e.g., BON standards, FERPA ). Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: Knowledge of effective teaching and evaluation strategies. Knowledge of and commitment to diversity, equity, and inclusion. Effective verbal and written communication skills. Ability to be self-directed and solve problems effectively and efficiently. Strong organizational skills. Positive and professional demeanor. Required Qualifications: Graduate degree in nursing from an accredited college or university and from a nursing education program that is accredited by a nursing education accreditation body recognized by the United States Department of Education; OR a bachelor’s degree in nursing from an accredited college or university and from a nursing education program that is accredited by a nursing education accreditation body recognized by the United States Department of Education, and a graduate degree in a health or education related field from an accredited college or university. Appropriate experience/credentials relating to the subject matter in relation to the assignment. Legally eligible to work in the United States on hire date AND legally authorized to work in the United States for Pacific Lutheran University without sponsorship. Finalist applicants must satisfactorily complete pre-employment background checks. Preferred Qualifications: Three years working in the area of specialty. Previous teaching experience in nursing. Specialty certification. Work Conditions: Conditions vary by course and include a variety of clinical practice settings. Physical Requirements: Depending in the assigned clinical teaching, may require prolonged standing and walking significant distances. Special Instructions to Applicants: Applications are considered throughout the academic year and reviewed based on programmatic needs. If a review of your application indicates a match with our current needs, you will be contacted by phone or email for an interview. Applicants should submit a resume/cv by clicking on the “Apply” link above. Clearly indicate formal education and areas of practice expertise, as well as any specialized training and/or relevant certifications. For more information, please contact SarahAnn McFadden at mcfaddsm@plu.edu Other Information: Pacific Lutheran University purposefully integrates the liberal arts, professional studies and civic engagement in the beautiful Pacific Northwest. Our faculty lead with a bold commitment to expanding well-being, opportunity, and justice and instill this commitment in their students. PLU educators work to create an environment where students feel heard and valued, nurturing lifelong learners who aren’t afraid to do things differently to make the world better for everyone. PLU supports faculty as they help students turn ambition into purpose, skills into tools, and their caring into a transformative force. Because PLU is a community committed to the creation and advancement of knowledge through diversity and inclusion, the university actively recruits a faculty, staff, and student body from all religions, races, socioeconomic groups, sexual orientations, and gender expressions and from all over the world. For the 2024–25 academic year, 40.6 percent of PLU undergraduate students are from the first generation in their families to attend college; 48.0 percent self-identify as students of color; and 34.2 percent are Pell Grant–eligible. Offering distinctive opportunities for global engagement, collaborative student-faculty research, and purposeful learning, our faculty guide 2,700 students from all faiths and backgrounds to discern their purpose through relevant coursework, inclusive mentoring, and internships at world-class Puget Sound-area businesses and institutions. PLU is located on a 156-acre woodland campus in the Parkland neighborhood of Tacoma, Washington and about 40 minutes south of downtown Seattle. The campus reflects consciously green practices and is in proximity to the natural beauty of Mount Rainier, the Cascade and Olympic mountain ranges, and the Puget Sound. PLU administration supports and respects the strong culture of faculty-shared governance by working closely and collaboratively with faculty leadership to sustain and strengthen existing structures and procedures while working together on key institutional, academic and curricular decisions. PLU is an equal opportunity employer and welcomes applications from members of historically underrepresented and marginalized groups, women, veterans, persons with disabilities, and others who would bring broadly diverse perspectives, experiences, and backgrounds in educational, research, or other work activities. For general application questions or if you need accommodations at any point in the application and/or interview process, please contact the Human Resources Office at 253-535-7185 or humr@plu.edu . Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Are you legally eligible to work in the United States on your hire date AND legally authorized to work in the United States for Pacific Lutheran University without sponsorship? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae/Resume Optional Documents Copyright © 2019 Pacific Lutheran University. All rights reserved. 12180 Park Ave. S Tacoma, WA 98447 (253) 535-7185 www.plu.edu To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://employment.plu.edu/postings/8894": {
      "title": "PLU",
      "description": "PLU - Applicant Portal | Budget &amp; Administrative Manager Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help PLU Home Budget & Administrative Manager Below you will find the details for the position including any supplementary documentation and questions you should review before applying for the opening. Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Posting Details Posting Number: 0603091 Recruitment Type: Open to All Applicants Position Title: Budget & Administrative Manager Position Type: Staff Benefits Status: Benefits Eligible FLSA: Non-Exempt Hiring Range: $20.75-$22.31 per hour, plus excellent benefits. Location Tacoma, WA 98447. Department: College of Professional Studies Posting Date: 08/21/2026 Closing Date When a sufficient number of qualified applicants have been identified. Work Schedule: Full-time, Monday-Friday, 8am-5pm General Description: The Budget and Administrative Manager provides multifaceted support for operations and budgets within the College of Professional Studies, with the main functions being primarily in the School of Music, Theatre & Dance ( SMTD ) and Partnerships and Professional Development ( PPD ) in the School of Education. This position establishes and maintains a professional presence on campus and in the extended community. Reports to the College Dean, with dotted lines to the Associate Dean of the School of Music, Theatre & Dance and Executive Director of Education Leadership Programs. Essential Functions and Responsibilities: Assist the Associate Dean ( SMTD ) and Executive Director of Educational Leadership Programs with the development of the annual budgets. Supervise the processing of all purchase transactions, income, and maintenance of internal bookkeeping systems in SMTD and PPD . Reconcile expenses and purchase card transactions. Maintain shadow budgets for all areas in SMTD and PPD . Produce income, expense, and statistical reports as requested by the College Dean, Associate Dean, or Executive Director. Assist with operations management in SMTD and PPD . Serve as accounts payable and accounts receivable liaison with the Business Office and Provost Office. Support the SMTD contingent faculty & PPD instructors, including the preparation and distribution of contracts and payroll preparation. Assist with the development of budgets for new PPD programs. Collaborate with staff in the operation of ticket pre-sales, including annual Christmas concert sales. Prepare deposits and track SMTD ticket sales, fees, income, and related taxes and fees. Serve as a representative on special projects and cross-functional campus committees. Interpret and implement policies and procedures. Research and propose appropriate policy and procedure revisions and produce reports. Provide administrative support as needed by faculty and staff, including assistance with the development and tracking of performance and outreach project budgets. Hire, supervise, and train SMTD student office assistants. Assist faculty members with the student hiring process as needed and review and approve monthly time sheets. Manage unit score and music performance recording libraries. Manage databases for SMTD projects (Christmas concerts, faculty-sponsored music workshops, festivals, and honor ensembles). Assist with the coordination of facility and equipment needs, maintenance, and repair. Perform other duties as assigned. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: Excellent communication (written and verbal) and interpersonal skills. Ability to use initiative and independent judgment in non-routine matters. Knowledge of and commitment to diversity, equity, and inclusion. Excellent organizational and multitasking skills, with a keen attention to detail. Excellent quantitative skills. Ability to employ a professional and cooperative work ethic. Full technical proficiency with Microsoft Office Suite, Google Workspace for Education, email, and the internet. Ability to adapt to changing environments. Ability to work in a fast-paced office environment involving many competing tasks. Ability to maintain confidentiality and interact with students, staff, administration, faculty, parents and community members in an effective and appropriate manner. Required Qualifications: Two years of job-related office experience. Education may substitute for some experience. Experience in budget planning and analysis, event management, and/or office management. High school diploma or GED . Finalist applicants must satisfactorily complete a pre-employment background check. Preferred Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree. Work experience in a university setting. Experience with, appreciation for, and an understanding of the arts and education. Work Conditions: Physical Requirements: Special Instructions to Applicants: Other Information: PLU is a small, private university where caring means more than kindness and consideration — it means a bold commitment to expanding well-being, opportunity, and justice. Because PLU is a community committed to the creation and advancement of knowledge through diversity and inclusion, the university actively recruits a diverse faculty, staff, and student body. PLU is an equal opportunity employer and welcomes applications from members of historically underrepresented and minoritized groups, women, veterans, persons with disabilities, and others who would bring broadly diverse perspectives, experiences, and backgrounds in educational, research or other work activities. PLU offers an excellent benefits package including tuition remission for employees and their dependents, retirement plan, medical, dental, paid vacation and sick leave, major holidays off (including Christmas/New Year’s break), additional flexible time off, and many other great university benefits. PLU is a qualifying employer under the Public Service Loan Forgiveness Program as a 501©(3) tax-exempt organization. Click here for detailed benefits information. For general application questions or if you need accommodations at any point in the application and/or interview process, please contact the Human Resources Office at 253-535-7185 or humr@plu.edu . Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Do you have a High School Diploma or GED? Yes No * Are you 18 years of age or older? Yes No * Are you legally eligible to work in the United States on your hire date AND legally authorized to work in the United States for Pacific Lutheran University without sponsorship now and indefinitely into the future? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter Resume List of Three References Optional Documents Copyright © 2019 Pacific Lutheran University. All rights reserved. 12180 Park Ave. S Tacoma, WA 98447 (253) 535-7185 www.plu.edu To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
